33
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:46,240
Very much from inception, actually.

34
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:50,240
Mini Pay, first of all, is important to understand as an opera product, an opera browser company.

35
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:55,000
I've been working at Opera for a number of years and was part of the initial team that

36
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,520
built this Web3 wallet back in 2018.

37
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:01,880
It was a very early attempt at this hybrid browser wallet product.

38
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:06,560
So I was very involved in that, left for a few years, but then I was keen to get back

39
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:09,200
and to create something very different.

40
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,680
A wallet that was using a lot of the same tech, but gave a very different experience

41
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:18,760
and really focused on simplicity rather than trying to do it all as it can easily happen

42
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:20,280
in crypto.

43
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:24,800
So it was something of a crypto native wallet to start with, which is different from today's

44
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:25,800
product?

45
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,360
Well, it was very much, think of it as the built-in metamask product.

46
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,960
So instead of having an extension, you had a browser that could do it all, very much

47
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:35,320
for crypto natives.

48
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:40,080
And Mini Pay is really trying to be something else, something for a much wider audience,

49
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:44,800
really centered around this payment with stablecoins use case, but leveraging much of the same

50
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:46,360
tech, actually.

51
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:52,040
The Mini Pay product as exists today, did that evolve because that's where you saw that

52
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,760
there was going to be market appetite for the product?

53
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:59,640
Or was it that you had a vision beforehand that that's where crypto was going to go and

54
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:03,120
stablecoins had a more wide audience use case?

55
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:06,600
So it really started as looking what's in front of us.

56
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:12,420
Uprise, by now, is a 30-year-old company and really focused since inception around access

57
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:17,080
to information in all kinds of contexts, pre-smartphones to up until now with both

58
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,220
desktop and mobile products.

59
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:24,740
And over the years, we've grown to having a large audience, large user base in many

60
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:28,860
countries, a big amount of users also on the African continent.

61
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:33,200
And we started seeing signs about two years ago that, okay, there's a lot of appetite

62
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,260
for dollar products and stablecoins versus overall crypto assets.

63
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:42,300
So we started thinking, okay, we have this large distribution, we have this natural demand

64
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:44,980
for dollar-based products.

65
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:50,280
We have technology to build a wallet to deal with those crypto dollars.

66
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,840
So let's see what we can build.

67
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:57,020
And learning from existing systems for mobile money on the continent that are very similar

68
00:03:57,020 --> 00:04:01,620
to Venmo, but things like Empest, things like Airtel Money, really sending value with phone

69
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:05,880
numbers and giving access to money versus giving access to information is where the

70
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:06,880
idea started.

71
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,020
And then it was about, okay, how do we build this?

72
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:11,700
What is the core user experience?

73
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,020
Is it a self-custody product?

74
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:14,580
Is it a custodial product?

75
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:17,020
And how do we bridge the gap to local currencies?

76
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:19,380
It's a big part of the challenge.

77
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:23,520
And early on, did you see people discovering stablecoins like you mentioned because they

78
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:26,860
were crypto users already and saw it on Binance?

79
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:31,900
Or was there more of a self-service aspect where people found that stablecoins existed

80
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:33,340
even if they weren't crypto users?

81
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:35,980
Or how was that early discovery happening?

82
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:40,940
This crypto audience that was already familiar with stablecoins was present then and it's

83
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:46,780
present now and it's growing, but there's a much bigger pie, if you will, of just access

84
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:47,780
to dollars.

85
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:52,700
And in a lot of those countries where MiniPay started, there's natural demand for dollar

86
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:53,700
things.

87
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:56,700
The dollar is very much seen as a feature rather than a bug.

88
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:01,980
So having a product that leverages that inherent demand is always a good place to start.

89
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:04,900
Obviously, now we're also growing outside of Africa.

90
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:09,580
We just recently launched our mobile app as a standalone MiniPay app, which delivers

91
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:10,840
the same experience.

92
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:13,720
Not for a global audience outside Africa.

93
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:17,320
And on that note, can you touch on how are people using MiniPay?

94
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:18,440
You alluded to it a bit.

95
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:20,200
And where is it being used today?

96
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:22,680
You mentioned Africa's initial nexus.

97
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:27,680
So Samsara in Africa is a big part of the user base because we started MiniPay as a

98
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,580
bundle product in the Opera Mini browser.

99
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:34,920
So Opera Mini is a household name on the continent.

100
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:39,680
And bundling MiniPay within it made sense because data usage is a big concern, which

101
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:42,440
is also why Opera Mini is popular.

102
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:45,160
Bundling means that people don't have to get another app.

103
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:50,360
They're in the same user experience, visual language environment, and it's a trusted

104
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:51,360
place.

105
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:55,800
That was our initial starting point of bundling Opera Mini and growing in that subsurface

106
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:56,800
region.

107
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:03,000
Now we're taking that same experience and taking it globally, both on iOS and Android,

108
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,360
and integrating through that with new partners that allow us to bridge with the traditional

109
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:09,000
financial system.

110
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:12,920
Are people keeping large balances for a long time in MiniPay?

111
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:14,680
Are they moving money quickly?

112
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:16,600
How are their behaviors?

113
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:20,800
What we see is the velocity is very high because it's a payment-based product.

114
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,480
It's not a holding wallet.

115
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:24,360
It's very dollar focused.

116
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:27,440
So we see high velocity on those payments.

117
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:32,380
And because these payments are basically frictionless and essentially free, and we have all these

118
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:37,360
ways to make your balance useful by being able to pay merchants and withdraw to local

119
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:40,200
currency that a lot of the utilities is from that.

120
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:46,720
And I think overall, that's a part of the product that is typically underdeveloped.

121
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:52,360
Crypto wallet products is the off-frame part because typically crypto has been about speculation.

122
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:55,040
It's been about number goes up is the use case.

123
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:00,520
So you let money there and withdrawal and connecting to the fiat part on the way out

124
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:02,520
was really much not thought about.

125
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:06,920
So it's something we had to build oftentimes together with new partners to unlock because

126
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:10,560
there hasn't been really anyone focusing on that so far.

127
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:11,560
I agree.

128
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,480
I think the off-frame piece is actually a gating factor for people not putting more

129
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:19,520
money into systems like this because they have their working expenses and they're going

130
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:23,360
to need to have instant integration to get money into traditional systems.

131
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:26,080
And I think that has been a place that's lacked.

132
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:31,400
Building a stablecoin product where you stay in stables is trivial.

133
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:35,400
Anyone can do that because it's blockchain native and makes a lot of assumptions about

134
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:37,760
what the user knows and what the user has.

135
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:42,960
But building a stablecoin product that is self-custody but also connects with the fiat

136
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:48,280
world on both ends, that's quite hard and it hasn't been done at a large scale before.

137
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:53,360
So the payments that you're seeing people make, is it mostly into traditional accounts

138
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:57,080
that vendors will have or into traditional accounts that other people will have or is

139
00:07:57,080 --> 00:08:02,080
it mini pay to mini pay wallet or is it mini pay to Binance or what most of them look like?

140
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:05,560
So a lot of the activity right now is just first party withdrawal.

141
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:10,600
So you're withdrawing to yourself, you're making your balance go from stables into your

142
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:12,320
preferred local currency.

143
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:16,440
But there's a growing trend of third party payments to merchants, to pay bills, to pay

144
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:17,720
for anything really.

145
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:22,480
And that's where I think the most exciting connectivity part is, is where we connect

146
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:27,360
the stablecoin balance to instant payment methods around the world to make this balance

147
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:28,920
useful everywhere.

148
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,480
Which stablecoins are you offering to users?

149
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:33,880
So we support three dollar stablecoins today.

150
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:41,060
We support USDT, Tether, USDC, and CUSD, which is a sell-o specific dollar stablecoin.

151
00:08:41,060 --> 00:08:44,480
Mini pay also, as you mentioned, is a sell-o product.

152
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:49,200
We've chosen sell-o for a few reasons that I can go into later if you want.

153
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:54,040
But building on sell-o has allowed us to also build an experience that works well in low

154
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:57,600
resource environments and is very efficient overall.

155
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:01,700
Has the stablecoin landscape evolved as you would have expected when you started building

156
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:02,700
mini pay?

157
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:05,720
And in that vein, would you have expected those to be the stablecoins or do you think

158
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:08,640
there would be other stablecoins that would be at scale now?

159
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:13,960
Well, it's certainly changed in the past two years from a regulatory point of view, from

160
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:17,180
an awareness and acceptance point of view.

161
00:09:17,180 --> 00:09:21,320
The word stablecoin, sometimes I'm a fan of it, sometimes not so much.

162
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:26,160
I think as an industry, it's probably an exercise there to define exactly that term.

163
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:31,360
Stablecoin is also a good one, but the market has changed from that aspect.

164
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:36,360
Now I think a big challenge when you build a product that supports multiple stablecoins

165
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:40,200
is how do you tell the user, is this a conscious choice?

166
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:41,520
Is this a market-based choice?

167
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:45,800
Like let's say the local fiat partners use one in particular, then obviously that's the

168
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:47,120
one you'll need to use.

169
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:51,220
So interoperability is something that I hope would have come further.

170
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,960
Between stablecoins, between blockchains?

171
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:59,400
Between stablecoins and blockchain interoperability is one topic, but I don't think anyone benefits

172
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:02,360
from having a hundred different kinds of dollars.

173
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:05,160
Add some letters after USD or before.

174
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:10,200
I think from an adoption perspective, it creates challenges if things are not interoperable.

175
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:14,260
So we've done some new stuff in MiniPay to alleviate some of those problems.

176
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:20,000
So you can, for example, merge your USDC, USDT and CUSD balances with just a drag and

177
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:21,000
drop.

178
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:24,640
You don't really have to think about managing your balance separately.

179
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:28,840
They're all essentially treated like one common balance that you can combine.

180
00:10:28,840 --> 00:10:30,000
That makes sense.

181
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,120
What offerings are you getting requested for the platform?

182
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:34,120
Are people content saving in dollars?

183
00:10:34,120 --> 00:10:38,080
Do they want interest-bearing accounts or anything of that sort?

184
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:44,400
So what we're hearing is that savings and yield opportunities is more of a nice to have

185
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:45,800
than a must have.

186
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:50,880
I think if you look at some of these sub-Saharan African countries, the fintech marketing there

187
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:57,000
will shout for 10%, 15%, 20% interest rates, yield with local currency.

188
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,840
And from a marketing perspective, that's very attractive.

189
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:04,760
So if you have a product in the market that says, yeah, we have 4% or 5% treasury level

190
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:09,440
yield, just from a billboard point of view, it's not as attractive, even though in nature

191
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:13,360
it is very different and competitive, the marketing doesn't really resonate.

192
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:17,520
So I think there's a challenge there, but also we don't want to be in a situation where

193
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:19,360
your balance is locked up.

194
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:20,720
The thing is called MiniPay.

195
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:26,360
We're working so hard to facilitate paying both MiniPay users and existing systems.

196
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:31,720
Having that balance locked up in a yield product, even with low friction, we can go against

197
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:32,720
that.

198
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:33,720
So we're looking at that.

199
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:35,760
We're looking at what are the possible solutions there.

200
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:39,160
It's something that users are asking, but it's not the main value prop for us.

201
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:46,320
So our value prop is really around that connectivity and ease of use for this new P2P money technology.

202
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:50,760
Did people readily trusted what MiniPay was doing early on in these regions who might

203
00:11:50,760 --> 00:11:53,520
not have accessed a similar product in the past?

204
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:58,920
That's an interesting question because we started MiniPay, as I said, within Opera Mini.

205
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:04,760
So people trust Opera Mini for accessing information and now they're being exposed to this new

206
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:07,760
product within the same environment.

207
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:12,600
What we found is that even though you have brand trust, building an app that deals with

208
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:15,920
money requires you to upgrade that trust.

209
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:20,300
People have different expectations when they deal with an app that deals with money.

210
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:24,640
So even though we started with brand trust, you need to work for it.

211
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:29,160
For a lot of people, having money in their hand, buying money in their pocket is ultimately

212
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:32,040
the ultimate test to know if this is real.

213
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:39,060
So that's why also having really solid local off-ramp to local currency is part of building

214
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:43,900
that trust because if they cannot see it in their existing system and pay for stuff

215
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:47,380
that they do on a weekly basis, then kind of misses the point.

216
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:49,660
It's either not useful or not trusted.

217
00:12:49,660 --> 00:12:52,700
And what do you think happens to local currency in that wake?

218
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:54,620
So I think it's to be seen.

219
00:12:54,620 --> 00:13:00,220
I think this technology is very powerful and making it accessible through such a simple

220
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:02,780
interface is something quite novel.

221
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:08,540
We have to see also what now from the regulatory aspect of dollar stablecoins becomes more

222
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:09,920
concrete.

223
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:11,220
What will other countries do?

224
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:16,980
Will that accelerate other jurisdictions to adopt stablecoin frameworks and allow local

225
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:20,100
stables to thrive, which is not the case today?

226
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:21,100
We'll see.

227
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:25,060
But at the end of the day, connecting blockchain rails to traditional rails, I think will be

228
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:30,200
a relevant problem for the coming years and products like ours will try to fill that gap.

229
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:34,340
Can you speak more on local stablecoins and the extent to which that's been a focus of

230
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:35,340
opera?

231
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:37,220
Because I think that's a segment of the market people overlook.

232
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:42,220
Yeah, it's overlooked because all the eyes are being drawn to the dollar, like in a way

233
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:44,060
the dollar is the best meme.

234
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:45,420
The one everyone wants.

235
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:46,420
Yes.

236
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:48,620
So there's a marketing aspect to it.

237
00:13:48,620 --> 00:13:52,940
It's hard to just grab attention for local currency on the blockchain.

238
00:13:52,940 --> 00:13:57,740
But I think if you ask most people, they don't like converting currencies.

239
00:13:57,740 --> 00:14:02,340
It's not a pleasant experience because oftentimes you have big number, it becomes small number.

240
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:06,060
You have, let's say, 100 shillings becoming $10, let's say.

241
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:09,780
You always have this impression that you're losing somehow.

242
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:15,580
But if you have a product, I think where local stables are super interesting and valuable

243
00:14:15,580 --> 00:14:19,700
from user perspective, if the number is the same, if you can open the app and say, okay,

244
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:24,260
I want to put 1,000 shillings in this and it's 1,000 shilling in the app, then you feel

245
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:26,100
like I didn't lose anything on the way in.

246
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:30,480
Because I think as crypto people, we've been trained to this is how it works and you lose

247
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:32,300
on the way in and there's fees and all that.

248
00:14:32,340 --> 00:14:37,260
But if you can create this one-to-one experience with local stables, I think that's a big motivator

249
00:14:37,260 --> 00:14:38,260
for adopting those.

250
00:14:38,260 --> 00:14:41,340
Then you can go to dollar stables if you want.

251
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:45,020
There's a mental barrier when the shillings can only be off-chain and the dollars can

252
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:47,460
only be on-chain as opposed to having them in the same account.

253
00:14:47,460 --> 00:14:48,460
Yeah.

254
00:14:48,460 --> 00:14:53,100
So I think if everything's on-chain and you can come in one-to-one with your local currency,

255
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:56,300
then it's just psychologically a better experience.

256
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:58,420
Then you can do whatever you want with it.

257
00:14:58,420 --> 00:15:02,700
Having this FX on the way in, I think some people will feel very good about it.

258
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:05,860
But a lot of people just would prefer to see the same number.

259
00:15:05,860 --> 00:15:09,420
I would think regulators would be promoting local stable coins, but I haven't seen it

260
00:15:09,420 --> 00:15:10,420
much yet.

261
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:11,420
Yeah.

262
00:15:11,420 --> 00:15:15,740
I haven't seen either and I think in a lot of ways, the US leads the way there.

263
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:19,940
And if there's concrete developments there in the near future, maybe that's going to

264
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:22,460
accelerate other people to look into that.

265
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:27,260
How have regulators interacted with Opera historically and with Opera Mini Pay?

266
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:31,280
So Mini Pay is just like a browser, it's a pure technology product.

267
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:33,340
So browsers allow you to browse the internet.

268
00:15:33,340 --> 00:15:36,300
It supports a few protocols to do that.

269
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:44,360
Mini Pay is a way to interact with these new protocols, these new networks where the information

270
00:15:44,360 --> 00:15:45,440
is money.

271
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:49,860
So from a conceptual point of view, these are equivalent products, these are pure technology

272
00:15:49,860 --> 00:15:50,860
products.

273
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:53,980
We're a self-custody product, so people control their funds.

274
00:15:53,980 --> 00:15:57,300
They can export their keys to other products.

275
00:15:57,300 --> 00:16:03,940
And the regulatory aspect to Mini Pay really lies on the connection to the fiat world.

276
00:16:03,940 --> 00:16:08,820
So everywhere Mini Pay is available, we have one or more partners that allow us to bridge

277
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:13,140
that gap from the traditional world to blockchain.

278
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:18,580
And that's where the regulatory and compliance requirements come in to either deposit or

279
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,620
withdraw into Mini Pay.

280
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:24,860
Do you see Mini Pay functioning like a bank at any point in time?

281
00:16:24,860 --> 00:16:29,700
Are embedded financial services, say a mortgage on a home, something that you guys will aspire

282
00:16:29,700 --> 00:16:31,200
to provide?

283
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:35,500
Mini Pay is, it's interesting you asked that because yes, it's a very simple app that allows

284
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:38,820
you to access dollars and send dollars with phone numbers.

285
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:40,500
That's the first level of it.

286
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:41,500
The basis.

287
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:46,420
And we have this fiat connectivity on both sides, but we also allow third-party developers

288
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:49,940
to build mini apps that are integrated in the wallet.

289
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:55,300
So things like you're suggesting could be built by a third party that has their own

290
00:16:55,300 --> 00:17:02,460
company, their own brand that can get distribution through this easy to use dollar wallet.

291
00:17:02,460 --> 00:17:06,980
So that's something that we're really pushing for this year is how do we make that developer

292
00:17:06,980 --> 00:17:08,360
experience better?

293
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:13,740
How do we make these feel more integrated so that users don't feel lost?

294
00:17:14,140 --> 00:17:20,220
Because a lot of these crypto native products are not really taught for a non-crypto audience.

295
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:25,260
So when you bring them in Mini Pay, you need to rethink from which words we're using to

296
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:29,700
describe this and what interface challenges we have.

297
00:17:29,700 --> 00:17:35,380
So we hope that third-party developers build mini apps that really expand the use cases

298
00:17:35,380 --> 00:17:39,820
and that we can help drive the distribution and users to those products.

299
00:17:39,820 --> 00:17:45,700
Do you think that banks and other local financial institutions will in a way be forced to interact

300
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:48,540
with stablecoins and provide stablecoin offerings?

301
00:17:48,540 --> 00:17:52,060
Absolutely, because at the end of the day, it's about efficiency.

302
00:17:52,060 --> 00:17:54,580
And banks are in the business of making money.

303
00:17:54,580 --> 00:17:59,620
There's a more efficient way to make money than there's a natural incentive to do that.

304
00:17:59,620 --> 00:18:06,140
So I think it's inevitable that more banks will integrate stablecoin technology somehow.

305
00:18:06,140 --> 00:18:10,740
And I think we're probably going to benefit from that as a side effect, because then they

306
00:18:10,740 --> 00:18:15,700
will be treated more like money from the financial institution's point of view.

307
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:17,820
They will be hopefully more interoperable.

308
00:18:17,820 --> 00:18:22,900
The on and off ramp will feel more natural, like a traditional fintech flow versus crypto

309
00:18:22,900 --> 00:18:23,900
on ramp.

310
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:27,420
So I think we will get the side benefits of that.

311
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:32,020
With that in mind, what do you think are the biggest areas where we still need improvement?

312
00:18:32,020 --> 00:18:34,220
Is it card payment integration?

313
00:18:35,020 --> 00:18:39,980
You mentioned off-ramps, but what's preventing this adoption from happening faster already?

314
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:45,480
So the part that I'm most excited about, and I think is being underlooked, is third-party

315
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:48,700
payments using local payment methods.

316
00:18:48,700 --> 00:18:54,680
So what I mean by that is being able to use your stablecoin balance and pay directly to

317
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:56,780
whatever the local relevant method is.

318
00:18:56,780 --> 00:18:58,740
For example, in Brazil, that's fixed.

319
00:18:58,740 --> 00:19:00,580
In the Philippines, that's Gcash.

320
00:19:00,580 --> 00:19:02,460
In Kenya, that's M-Pesa.

321
00:19:02,460 --> 00:19:03,460
Things like that.

322
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:09,220
A lot of countries have these dominant instant payment methods, and being able to pay directly

323
00:19:09,220 --> 00:19:14,940
to those, pay directly to people, pay directly to merchants, and allow stablecoins to connect

324
00:19:14,940 --> 00:19:19,300
to these existing network effects within each country, I think that's being overlooked.

325
00:19:19,300 --> 00:19:24,220
A concrete example of that is, if you land in Kenya today, you have two choices.

326
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:29,180
Either you queue up to the Safaricom booth to buy a Safaricom SIM card and top it up

327
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:34,180
with cash, or you just have MiniPay, which you've topped up in your home country, but

328
00:19:34,180 --> 00:19:36,260
you can pay, both ways are equivalent.

329
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:41,380
You can now pay any M-Pesa merchant with this app, and the same story can be replicated

330
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:42,380
globally.

331
00:19:42,380 --> 00:19:46,980
So cards, I think, it grabs a lot of attention because we, in our countries, and I think

332
00:19:46,980 --> 00:19:50,240
you're in the US, we all use cards on a daily basis.

333
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:56,220
But a lot of countries have either skipped cards or have found more direct alternatives.

334
00:19:56,340 --> 00:19:59,940
Being able to connect directly to that is, I think, the most overlooked, and we have

335
00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:02,620
some pretty exciting stuff coming in that regard.

336
00:20:02,620 --> 00:20:07,820
And as those systems continue to evolve, is dollar hegemony a long-term byproduct of this

337
00:20:07,820 --> 00:20:12,740
stablecoin adoption, or where do we land in terms of the distribution of global monetary

338
00:20:12,740 --> 00:20:13,740
systems?

339
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:15,460
Oh, that's a Nick Carter question.

340
00:20:15,460 --> 00:20:17,980
Yeah, I stole it from him.

341
00:20:17,980 --> 00:20:21,380
So look, I'm not a macro guy.

342
00:20:21,380 --> 00:20:24,340
I think it really depends on what the response will be.

343
00:20:24,340 --> 00:20:31,380
Once the US passes some kind of stablecoin legislation, and basically, and trenches allow

344
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:35,180
all the financial institutions to interact with this in a way they haven't been able

345
00:20:35,180 --> 00:20:38,860
to do before, it's all going to be down to the response of all the other countries.

346
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:45,420
Are they going to just sit and wait and let that USD stable adoption continue to grow?

347
00:20:45,420 --> 00:20:50,340
Or are they going to be aggressively promoting local alternatives?

348
00:20:50,340 --> 00:20:51,340
It remains to be seen.

349
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:56,860
I think it's more of a politics and regulatory questions than a technology question.

350
00:20:56,860 --> 00:20:57,860
That makes sense.

351
00:20:57,860 --> 00:21:00,500
I think it's to be determined where we go a little bit.

352
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:02,020
But the dollar is the best meme.

353
00:21:02,020 --> 00:21:03,020
It is.

354
00:21:03,020 --> 00:21:04,220
And memes are powerful.

355
00:21:04,220 --> 00:21:05,660
So we'll see.

356
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:07,100
And there's a lot of momentum.

357
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:08,100
There is a lot of momentum.

